import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Why the Course Looks Like This","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"research/design-rationale.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/design-rationale.md","filePath":"research/design-rationale.md"}'),o={name:"research/design-rationale.md"};function n(i,e,l,d,h,c){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="why-the-course-looks-like-this" tabindex="-1">Why the Course Looks Like This <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-course-looks-like-this" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why the Course Looks Like This&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>The main design decisions in Engineering Software with AI, the evidence behind each, and where each appears in the course. For instructors adapting it, for curriculum reviewers, and for students who want to know why they&#39;re asked to work this way. Evidence comes from the <a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html">November 2026 landscape report</a> and its three <a href="./">research notes</a>. Where a decision rests on practitioner consensus or a single course&#39;s experience rather than a study, the table says so.</p></blockquote><h2 id="the-short-version" tabindex="-1">The short version <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-short-version" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The short version&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Teach engineering judgment around agents, not the agents.</strong> Upper-division courses now agree on a shape: build or dissect an agent early, then work inside a real codebase; teach security of agents before giving them broad access; verify with measured test adequacy; review at scale; grade evidence of process rather than output (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#1-comparable-courses">report §1</a>). The research agrees with that shape: output rises with agents while quality signals worsen, benchmarks overstate capability, LLM-written tests agree with the code&#39;s bugs, and agents that read untrusted text can be steered (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#4-the-evidence-base">report §4</a>). And on a $0 budget the binding constraint is model capacity, so the course spreads work across free pools and a lab machine (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#5-the-0-tool-landscape">report §5</a>).</p><h2 id="how-strong-is-the-evidence" tabindex="-1">How strong is the evidence? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-strong-is-the-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How strong is the evidence?&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Randomized and preregistered studies</strong> exist for productivity (field experiments with 4,867 developers, <a href="https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/fpi/10.1287/mnsc.2025.00535" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Cui et al.</a>; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.00788" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Borg et al.</a>) but not for teaching practices in courses like this one.</li><li><strong>Large observational studies</strong> of repositories and PRs (Cursor adoption, AIDev, agentic PRs) show associations, not causes.</li><li><strong>Benchmark and security studies</strong> are mostly preprints, several from vendors; they point the same way.</li><li><strong>Course designs</strong> (UT Austin, Michigan, CMU, Stanford) are published syllabi and assignments; none has published outcome data for the labs this course borrows (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#7-gaps">report §7</a>). Where the course copies a course practice, the evidence is &quot;a strong course does it&quot;, and the table says so.</li></ul><h2 id="decisions-and-evidence" tabindex="-1">Decisions and evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#decisions-and-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decisions and evidence&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Where it appears</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>The triad, Specify · Delegate · Verify</strong>, decided before every agent task; the sibling course&#39;s <strong>Safe Loop</strong> as the inner loop; every claim tagged <strong>observed</strong> or <strong>inferred</strong></td><td>UT Austin CS 378 frames agentic work as deciding &quot;what you specify, what you delegate, and what you verify&quot; (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CS 378</a>); the observed/inferred discipline comes from Stanford CS146S. The Safe Loop is the lower-division course&#39;s workflow, so students arriving from it have a common vocabulary. <em>Course practice, not a controlled study.</em></td><td><a href="./../resources/safe-loop.html">Safe Loop page</a> · <a href="./../weeks/01-agentic-engineering/">week 1</a> · <a href="./../SYLLABUS.html">syllabus</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>The week order</strong>: agents (1–2) → context (3) → security (4) → specs (5) → verification (6–7) → legacy code (8) → review (9) → supply chain (10) → LLM features and evals (11–12) → parallel agents (13) → evidence and responsibility (14)</td><td>Each unit uses the previous one: security before teams give agents broad access; specs before tests; tests before refactoring; review once there are PRs to review; evals after there is a feature to evaluate; parallel agents only once review and CI exist to absorb their output. UT Austin and Michigan follow a similar arc (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#what-they-teach-and-in-what-order">report §1</a>). Parallelism comes late because &quot;Parallelize a task once you can name its merge point&quot; (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/schedule.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">UT schedule</a>) needs review and CI first.</td><td><a href="./../SYLLABUS.html">Syllabus</a> · <a href="./../">weeks/</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Run, read and extend a minimal agent in week 1, then study a real harness in week 2</strong></td><td>Michigan replaced its multi-week &quot;Build the agent&quot; lab and dropped a stand-alone eval suite after student feedback; its students wanted to learn to use tools well, and &quot;almost nobody asked what to build with it&quot; (<a href="https://github.com/eecs498-aase/materials/blob/HEAD/labs/Lab03/slides.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Lab03</a>). Reference agents are small: about 100 lines (<a href="https://github.com/SWE-agent/mini-swe-agent" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">mini-swe-agent</a>), under 400 (<a href="https://ampcode.com/how-to-build-an-agent" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ball</a>). A week spent running, reading and extending one makes the real harness legible; extending a real codebase serves the &quot;large codebase&quot; outcome.</td><td><a href="./../weeks/01-agentic-engineering/">Week 1</a> · <a href="./../weeks/02-inside-agents/">week 2</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Boyu-Zhang-UOI/eswa-mini-agent" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>mini-agent</code></a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Security in week 4</strong>, with an injection lab against the student&#39;s own agent</td><td>UT Austin teaches prompt injection, the lethal trifecta and sandboxing in week 3 (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/schedule.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">schedule</a>). Planted payloads made agentic editors run malicious commands up to 84% of the time (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22040" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Liu et al.</a>); one MCP server can supply all three legs of the trifecta (<a href="https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jul/6/supabase-mcp-lethal-trifecta/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Willison</a>). Defenses that hold are architectural (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.18813" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CaMeL</a>; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08837" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">design patterns</a>), so students build them: allowlist, approvals, egress block, quarantined reading. <em>No course has published outcome data for an injection lab.</em></td><td><a href="./../weeks/04-securing-agents/">Week 4</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Boyu-Zhang-UOI/eswa-injection-lab" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>injection-lab</code></a> · <a href="./../setup/safety-contract.html">safety contract</a> · <a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#prompt-injection-and-agent-incidents">case studies A</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Property-based testing (week 6), then mutation testing (week 7)</strong>, with mutation score, not coverage, as the adequacy measure</td><td>LLM oracles capture actual rather than expected behavior (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.21136" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Konstantinou et al.</a>); LLM tests rarely probe boundaries (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32548" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Shi et al.</a>); suites with full coverage can have very low mutation scores (<a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2506.02954v5" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MutGen</a>); mutation-guided generation works at Meta (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12862" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Foster et al.</a>). The <strong>ISSTA 2026 caveat</strong> is taught too: both measures are unreliable when the code may already be buggy (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.22880" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Zhao et al.</a>). Mutation testing of AI tests is &quot;an open niche&quot; with no published course results (<a href="./notes/courses-upper-division.html#q5-labs-that-worked-or-failed">courses notes, Q5</a>).</td><td><a href="./../weeks/06-tests-as-oracles/">Weeks 6</a>–<a href="./../weeks/07-test-adequacy/">7</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Boyu-Zhang-UOI/eswa-roomly" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>roomly</code></a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Check first</strong>: tests written before the code, in a separate session</td><td>Tests generated after faulty code caught 14% of its faults against 25% for independently generated ones (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05139" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Konstantinou, Tambon &amp; Papadakis</a>).</td><td><a href="./../resources/safe-loop.html">Safe Loop</a> · <a href="./../resources/agent-patterns.html#check-first">agent patterns</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Context engineering as a measured practice</strong>: write an <code>AGENTS.md</code>, then A/B-test whether an agent follows it</td><td>Agent-first repositories without a committed AI config saw complexity rise 53% vs 27% with one, and 73.8% of config files were never edited after the first commit (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.25241" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Denisov-Blanch et al.</a>, observational). Longer contexts degrade every model tested (<a href="https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Chroma</a>). Stanford CS146S assigns an <code>AGENTS.md</code> for a repository students didn&#39;t write (<a href="./notes/courses-upper-division.html#q2-team-projects-on-existing-open-source-codebases">courses notes</a>).</td><td><a href="./../weeks/03-context-engineering/">Week 3</a> · <a href="./../resources/agent-patterns.html">agent patterns</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>A real open-source codebase</strong>, by default an open-source coding agent</td><td>CMU 17-313 works in a class fork of opencode; Michigan in Aider&#39;s real codebase (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#2-team-projects-on-real-codebases">report §2</a>). An agent codebase teaches the course&#39;s subject twice. One default candidate was dormant at the last check, so selection uses explicit criteria and gates.</td><td>Codebase selection · <a href="./../TOOLS.html#the-class-codebase">Tool Handout: The class codebase</a> · <a href="./../project/class-codebase.html">project</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>The three-tier project</strong>: class fork (graded) → PR round across team forks → optional upstream PRs to a vetted list</td><td>CMU&#39;s class fork with snapshot grading; UT Austin&#39;s one-week PR round in classmates&#39; repositories; Stanford&#39;s partner projects (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#2-team-projects-on-real-codebases">report §2</a>). Maintainers in 2026 ban, restrict or throttle AI-assisted PRs, and platforms cap outside PRs (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#maintainers-ai-policies">case studies E</a>); one research group&#39;s conduct once got a university banned from the kernel (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#e8-hypocrite-commits-when-research-gets-an-institution-banned">case E8</a>).</td><td><a href="./../project/">Project</a> · <a href="./../project/pr-round.html">PR round</a> · <a href="./../project/contribution-rules.html">contribution rules</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Grade process, not merges</strong>; merges earn a small bonus and never a penalty</td><td>44% of pre-AI student PRs were accepted upstream, and the authors advise to &quot;avoid mandatory grading of whether pull requests are accepted&quot; (<a href="https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~weimerw/p/weimer-fse2023.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Fang et al.</a>). Rejection is a noisy label: only 35.7% of agentic-PR rejections were clear agent failures (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.22534" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Peralta et al.</a>). UT Austin&#39;s rule that &quot;feature count, repository size, token spend, prompt volume, and visual polish do not substitute for demonstrated behavior, reproducible checks… or honest limitations&quot; (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CS 378</a>).</td><td><a href="./../assessment/">Assessment</a> · <a href="./../assessment/rubrics.html">rubrics</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disclosure as engineering practice</strong>: <code>AI_LOG.md</code>, the <code>Assisted-by:</code> trailer, self-written PR descriptions</td><td>LLVM&#39;s policy uses <code>Assisted-by:</code> and asks for contributor-written PR descriptions (<a href="https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/llvm/docs/AIToolPolicy.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LLVM</a>); 51% of 118 policies require disclosure (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.16706" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hora &amp; Robbes</a>); detectors can&#39;t replace recorded attribution (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36229" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Horikawa et al.</a>). Projects disagree on the trailer (QEMU&#39;s proposal uses <code>AI-used-for:</code>; the kernel dropped its tag), so the course teaches the convention <em>and</em> the rule that upstream policy wins.</td><td><a href="./../templates/AI_LOG.html">AI_LOG template</a> · <a href="./../templates/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.html">PR template</a> · <a href="./../setup/safety-contract.html#11-disclose-ai-help">contract rule 11</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Review at scale measured against a defect key</strong> (precision and recall), with AI reviewers as subjects, not oracles</td><td>AI reviewers&#39; comments lead to changes less often than humans&#39; and their effect on cycle time goes both ways (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.18531" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Cihan et al.</a>; <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.01129v2" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Tantithamthavorn et al.</a>); agent-only-reviewed PRs merged 45.2% vs 68.4% (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.03196" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Chowdhury et al.</a>). In a course capstone, tooling reliability and instructions mattered more than the model, so the bot is piloted before students rely on it (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.23251" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Oliveira et al.</a>).</td><td><a href="./../weeks/09-review-at-scale/">Week 9</a> · <a href="./../templates/REVIEW.html">REVIEW template</a> · instructor guide</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Evals as experiments</strong>: κ and not just agreement, pass^k, error bars, recorded responses in CI; benchmarks as claims to interrogate</td><td>Criteria drift (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.12272" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Shankar et al.</a>); agreement hides judge gaps (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12624" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Thakur et al.</a>); error bars and paired comparisons (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00640" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Miller</a>); pass^k (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">τ-bench</a>); every major SWE benchmark overstates capability (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#benchmarks">report §4</a>). Michigan dropped a stand-alone eval project; the surviving form is small and comparative (<a href="./notes/courses-upper-division.html#q5-labs-that-worked-or-failed">courses notes, Q5</a>), so evals here are two weeks on one feature.</td><td><a href="./../weeks/11-llm-features/">Weeks 11</a>–<a href="./../weeks/12-evals/">12</a> · <a href="https://github.com/Boyu-Zhang-UOI/eswa-eval-harness" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>eval-harness</code></a> · <a href="./../templates/EVAL_PLAN.html">EVAL_PLAN template</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Specs that change mid-assignment</strong> (&quot;beats&quot;)</td><td>UT Austin&#39;s homeworks arrive &quot;in beats … the spec changes under you&quot; (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CS 378</a>). <em>Course practice.</em></td><td><a href="./../weeks/05-spec-driven-development/">Week 5</a> · <a href="./../templates/SPEC.html">SPEC template §8</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Due-date quizzes</strong>: 7 minutes, AI-free, at the start of the Seminar when an assignment is due, weeks 2–14</td><td>UT Austin&#39;s practice, with &quot;only a single browser tab open&quot; (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CS 378</a>). They check that the student understands the work just submitted, cheaply, every week. <em>Course practice; no outcome data published.</em></td><td><a href="./../assessment/quizzes.html">Quizzes</a> · instructor guide</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Oral exams</strong> (weeks 8 and 14, 15 minutes, individual, AI off) and a <strong>final presentation</strong> with a live demo</td><td>UT Austin grades its final with a live defense; Michigan&#39;s rule is &quot;if you can&#39;t explain it, you didn&#39;t build it&quot; (<a href="https://eecs498-aase.github.io/syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">AASE</a>). Higher confidence in AI predicts less critical thinking (<a href="https://advait.org/files/lee_2025_ai_critical_thinking_survey.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Lee et al.</a>), and students using agents understood less and extended their code worse without AI (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.26375" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Balepur et al.</a>): explanation has to be checked, not assumed.</td><td><a href="./../assessment/oral-exams.html">Oral exams</a> · <a href="./../project/final-presentation.html">final presentation</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>The graduate track</strong>: one paper presentation and one discussant role, a critique, and a $0 empirical study (about 20% of the CS [5xxx] grade)</td><td>UIdaho&#39;s joint-listed courses state &quot;Additional projects/assignments required for graduate credit&quot; (<a href="https://catalog.uidaho.edu/courses/cs/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">catalog</a>); peers use research notes, research projects and presentations worth about 15–25% (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#3-graduate-differentiation">report §3</a>). The rubric is CU Boulder&#39;s; the presenter–discussant structure is Cornell&#39;s. Nineteen papers suit presentations and five can be replicated at $0 (<a href="./../resources/reading-list.html#graduate-seminar-set">reading list</a>). No human-subjects work without IRB (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html#e8-hypocrite-commits-when-research-gets-an-institution-banned">case E8</a>).</td><td><a href="./../graduate/">Graduate track</a> · <a href="./../templates/STUDY_PROTOCOL.html">STUDY_PROTOCOL template</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>$0 to students, with a lab machine running local models</strong>, provider-swappable kits and planned fallbacks</td><td>Capacity, not tools, is scarce: Copilot Student is reported at about $2 a month against about $1.50 per default code review (<a href="./notes/tools-senior.html">tool notes</a>). UT Austin and Michigan reach $0 through institutional accounts or course credit, which this course can&#39;t assume. Michigan teaches on small local models on purpose (<a href="https://eecs498-aase.github.io/syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">AASE</a>). Local models fail silently without a 64K context (<a href="https://docs.ollama.com/context-length" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ollama</a>). 2025-era labs broke when free tiers closed in 2026 (<a href="./landscape-report-2026-11.html#5-the-0-tool-landscape">report §5</a>).</td><td><a href="./../TOOLS.html">Tool Handout</a> · <a href="./../setup/local-models.html">local models</a> · variants</td></tr><tr><td><strong>An agent safety contract</strong>, signed in week 1: approvals on, sandboxes for untrusted code, no real secrets near agents, scoped tokens, spending caps at zero, no unattended agents in other people&#39;s spaces</td><td>Each rule traces to an incident or study: agents deleting production data despite instructions, a public issue leaking a private repository, an agent editing its own permissions, agents publishing internal screenshots, malware hunting AI keys (<a href="./../resources/case-studies.html">case studies</a>). Agentic workflows&#39; default per-run cap exceeds a student&#39;s monthly allowance (<a href="./../TOOLS.html#free-infrastructure">Tool Handout: Free infrastructure</a>).</td><td><a href="./../setup/safety-contract.html">Safety contract</a> · <a href="./../setup/sandboxes.html">sandboxes</a> · <a href="./../setup/api-keys.html">API keys</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>H-1-a scheduling</strong>: no quiz, oral exam or anything exam-like in week 15; the final presentations in the finals-week exam period</td><td>UIdaho Regulation H-1-a: &quot;No quizzes or exams may be given in lecture-recitation periods during the week before finals week&quot;; H-1-b sets the final exam period. So oral exam 2 is in week 14, due-date quizzes stop at week 14, week 15 is an integration sprint and dry run, and graduate study talks (presentations, not exams) may run in week 15.</td><td><a href="./../SYLLABUS.html">Syllabus</a> · <a href="./../weeks/15-ship-and-reflect/">week 15</a> · <a href="./../weeks/16-final-presentations/">week 16</a></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Graded personal writing on Canvas</strong>, never in a public repository</td><td>Team forks are public (so CI and security tooling are free); reading responses, reflections, peer evaluations and graduate critiques are personal and stay private. Inherited from the sibling course.</td><td><a href="./../setup/accounts.html#canvas">Accounts</a> · <a href="./../assessment/">assessment</a></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="what-we-deliberately-did-not-do" tabindex="-1">What we deliberately did not do <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-we-deliberately-did-not-do" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What we deliberately did not do&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A multi-week build-your-own-agent project.</strong> Michigan tried and replaced it (<a href="https://github.com/eecs498-aase/materials/blob/HEAD/labs/Lab03/slides.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Lab03</a>).</li><li><strong>Required upstream merges, or required upstream PRs at all.</strong> The pre-AI merge rate was 44%, and 2026 maintainers are more cautious.</li><li><strong>A required paid subscription.</strong> UMD requires one (<a href="https://www.cs.umd.edu/class/fall2026/cmsc389A/Syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">389A</a>); this course&#39;s $0 rule rules it out, and department credits are an optional variant.</li><li><strong>AI detectors.</strong> Detection of agent commits reaches a precision of 0.332 at realistic prevalence (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36229" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Horikawa et al.</a>); the course asks students to explain instead.</li><li><strong>Grading the exact MCP result shape.</strong> The specification&#39;s <code>tools/call</code> result is on the roadmap for redesign (<a href="./notes/tools-senior.html#5-mcp-in-late-2026">tool notes</a>).</li><li><strong>Copilot as the main agent.</strong> Its student allowance can&#39;t carry agent work (<a href="./../TOOLS.html#the-github-student-developer-pack">Tool Handout: The GitHub Student Developer Pack</a>).</li></ul><h2 id="open-questions" tabindex="-1">Open questions <a class="header-anchor" href="#open-questions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Open questions&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Does a week with a small agent students run, read and extend, plus a real harness, teach agent internals better than either alone? No course has measured it.</li><li>Do injection labs change what students do later with agents, or only what they know?</li><li>Is mutation score a useful grading target for AI-written tests, given the ISSTA 2026 result? Graduate studies can help answer this (<a href="./../resources/reading-list.html#replicable-studies">replicable studies</a>).</li><li>How will 2026&#39;s maintainer policies affect optional upstream PRs? Record merge and response data each term (process data only, no study of maintainers).</li></ul><h2 id="how-to-update-the-course" tabindex="-1">How to update the course <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-to-update-the-course" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How to update the course&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A tool changed:</strong> update <a href="./../TOOLS.html">Tool Handout</a> only, and its date. If a lab can no longer run on any free tool, the lab changes.</li><li><strong>New evidence:</strong> add it to a dated note in <a href="./">notes/</a>, then to the reading list and, if it changes a decision, to this table.</li><li><strong>A new term:</strong> re-run the instructor&#39;s pre-term checklist, including the class-codebase gates.</li></ul>',14)])])}const p=t(o,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
